  "query_preview": "..\n\nSaturday through Sunday: A deep low pressure system right on the \ncoast of British Columbia will move southward and into the PNW in \nthe early hours of Saturday, bringing a chance for precipitation in \nthe mountains. The northern mountains and northern Cascade crest \nhave a 40 percent chance and above at seeing 0.10 inches of rain, \nwith those chances decreasing as you move into the ID Panhandle. \nInitially, models showed far SE Washington alongside the Panhandle \nhaving chances of rain, but models have since pushed the \nprecipitation further southeastward due to a dry slot formation. \nWith steep lapse rates and 200-300 J/kg of CAPE, the Cascades and \nnorthern mountains also have a chance at thunderstorms beginning mid-\nmorning. DCAPE values around 500 J/kg indicate that one of the \nimpacts will be gusty outflow winds. Other impacts include lightning \nand heavy rainfall with any cells that form. \n\nWith this frontal passage, expect widespread gusty winds. Much of \nthe basin, Spokane/CdA area, Palouse, Waterville Plateau, and \nCascade gaps have a 60 percent chance and above at seeing 30 mph \nwind gusts. This could result in blowing objects and trouble \ndriving, especially for high profile vehicles. Though RHs are above \n20 percent through much of the area, some fire weather concerns may \nexist, especially in places that won't receive rain. \n\nAdditionally, expect a drop in temperatures. From yesterday to \ntoday, temperatures will drop 5-7 degrees, with highs expected in \nthe low to mid 70s for much of the area. An important thing to keep \nan eye on will be sheltered valleys in the Idaho Panhandle and \nnorthern mountains. Models are further hinting at low temperatures \nSaturday into Sunday dropping into the mid-30s, which could lead to \nsome frost formation. Additionally, fog in these sheltered valleys \nis possible as well, with RHs remaining high due to that colder air \nstaying in the valleys. \n\nMonday through Friday: As the low moves out of the area, another low \nwill drop down off the PNW coast. There will be slight ridging \nbetween these lows, and this will lead to an increase in \ntemperatures back into the 80s for Monday and Tuesday. On Wednesday, \nthe clusters are in unanimous agreement that the low off coast will \ndeepen and move onshore. Models are showing higher chances for more \nwidespread precipitation, so stay tuned. The 6-10 and 8-14 Day \nOutlooks are also that we have higher chances for lower than normal \ntemperatures and higher than normal precipitation, so expect a major \npattern change over the weekend and next week. /AS",
